;;; A positive derived under `(absent (q one))` survives into a world that
;;; commits `(q one)` — regression `stale-naf-fork`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`), whose right answer is the refusal:
;;; *forbid* refuses the program (below).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(p one)` is derived at root because `(q one)` is absent. `p` and `q`
;;; together are a contradiction. The goal is `q`, so the rung proposes
;;; `(q one)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine said, before it refused ────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; As banked:
;;;
;;;   Solution k = 1, `{(p one)}` plus `(not (q one))`, `exhausted = true`
;;;
;;; Committing `(q one)` falsifies `derive-p`'s guard, but nothing retracts
;;; (docs/ein/reasoning/02-kb-evolution/03-absent-and-naf.md § What is not
;;; provided), so the fork held the stale `(p one)` beside `(q one)`, the
;;; clash fired, and the candidate was killed: the state `{(q one)}` was
;;; unreachable. R said the same at `c887a35b`: one state, ∅, root holding
;;; `(p one)`, and the fork `{(q one)}` dead, the one of the cube's two nodes
;;; holding a stale fact.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand: a reading ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Does a guard's `absent` premise bind the state the search ends in? Ein
;;; does not decide it: a node is a fork of root, an assumption on a program the check flags (Q-M1e2.2). The readings:
;;;
;;;   guards are premises   committing `(q one)` withdraws `(p one)`, the
;;;                         clash never fires, `{(q one)}` is alive, and root
;;;                         is not maximal: **`Solution`, k = 1, `{(q one)}`**.
;;;   append-only           the answer above was right: the fork with both is
;;;                         dead, and root is the solution.
;;;
;;; The program is stratified (`q` is no rule's conclusion), so the third
;;; reading — refuse what cannot be stratified — does not reach it.
;;;
;;; ── Refused: *forbid*, through a derived fact ──────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; *Forbid* (Q-M1e2.5): a refutation may not rest on an `absent` over a
;;; relation a commitment can still extend, because a state that has not yet
;;; decided it would be called dead, and a commitment would lift that; a
;;; refutation resting on an `absent` only through a fact a rule derived
;;; under it counts. Here `p-and-q-clash` reads `(p ?x)`, which `derive-p`
;;; derives under `(absent (q ?x))`, and `q` is a relation a commitment can
;;; extend; unfolded it is `(thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)) (q ?x) ⟹ (false)`. So
;;; the program is refused at load, every question about a commitment exits 1
;;; naming `p-and-q-clash`, `derive-p` and `q`, and the regression's right
;;; answer is the refusal. The two readings above are what a program that
;;; loads leaves undecided (Q-M1e2.2); this one does not load, and
;;; `features/07` keeps the question.
;;;
;;; `ein solve` in every mode, `ein oracle` and `ein render lattice` exit 1
;;; with that one line on stderr. `ein test` exits 2: here because no
;;; `:expect` is stated (*nothing to check*), and on a copy stating one
;;; because the file does not load. `ein saturate` saturates, deriving
;;; `(p one)`, and the views that take no universe (`render rules`,
;;; `constraints`, `program`, `kb`) draw.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
(relation thing T :why "{?3} is a thing")
(relation p T :why "p holds of {?3}")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?3}")
(thing one)
(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x) :why "{?x} is a thing and no q fact holds of it, so p holds of {?x}")
(rule p-and-q-clash ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (q ?x))
  :assert (false) :why "both p and q hold of {?x}, which cannot be")
(query :goal (q ?x))
